strycat@gmail.com wrote:
>
> So I walk into Njezjin's liquor emporium with a huge selection of
> potions.  Should I pick up each potion, find out the price, and then
> #name it "X zorkmids" or is that just a waste of time?

It's a matter of taste whether it's worth it to do that.  One thing
mentioned by others that is far more important with scrolls than
potions - All you need do is stand on the square and chat to
get its price.  There are scrolls that go poof when you pick them
up but not potions.

> Same question about the various bookstores?

More worth doing for scrolls and spellbooks than potions.

> This method of ID'ing stuff quickly becomes tedious and not fun (kinda
> like Sokoban only in a different way).

For potions I ask the price for a different reason.  Once I have a
pet in the shop it will tend to pick up the stuff closer to me and
it is more likely to leave the shop before dropping it.  So I pick up
the expensive potions and place them closer to me in the store
and move the cheaper or known ones deeper into the shop.

> Is there a better way to ID scrolls and potions?  I know I shouldn't
> just randomly quaff potions and read scrolls.  I've started to just
> wait until a I see potions used by monsters and ID them that way.
> Still it seems rather a poor way.  What am I missing?

If I have a place I can close the door and be alone in a room
I'll ID potions by quaffing them once I know they aren't cursed.  The
risky ones are sleep, paralysis and polymorph.  Unihorn dipping
first handles polymorph and each of the others know reduces the
chances.  By the time I know those three I might even quaff in
some large and empty room.

Scrolls to me are important enough to price ID.  Books I can
try - if it is hard to read I'll try again 10 levels later.  Both
scrolls
and books get the same higher-price-close-to-me trick.



